<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Set Up Your Godot Game World in Blender" description="Author a Godot level's sky color, gravity, background music, and load or unload events from Blender with Folded Paper Engine." summary="A level is more than its meshes. It has a mood, a sense of weight, a soundtrack, and an entrance. FPE lets the Blender scene describe those world-level decisions alongside the art that inspired them." relatedSlugs={["play-3d-audio-godot","change-scenes-load-levels-godot","build-levels-in-blender-for-godot"]} fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot">
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>You will make:</strong> a Blender-authored level that arrives in Godot with its intended sky, gravity, music, and lifecycle events.</div>
    <h2>Open the FPE Scene panel</h2><p>These settings belong to the Blender scene, not an individual prop. Start with a small level that already exports successfully; world settings should enrich a proven pipeline rather than obscure a broken one.</p>
    <h2>Set the world mood</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Sky Color</strong> to establish the level's background tone. Treat it as a starting environment color, then refine lighting and materials in context.</li><li>Set <strong>Gravity</strong> in meters per second squared. Use familiar gravity unless floatiness or heaviness is an intentional part of the world.</li><li>Choose <strong>Background Music</strong> using the path the audio resource will have inside the Godot project.</li><li>Set <strong>Background Music Volume</strong> in decibels. Preview the actual mix in Godot rather than choosing by number alone.</li></ol>
    <h2>Give the level an entrance and exit</h2><p><strong>Scene Load Events</strong> run commands as the level becomes active. Use them for deliberate initialization such as starting an animation, dispatching a named event, or establishing a camera beat. <strong>Scene Unload Events</strong> are for cleanup or a final transition action. Keep both lists short and meaningful; complex persistent state still belongs to Godot systems.</p>
    <h2>Verify the whole level</h2><ul><li>The sky matches the intended mood under the actual camera.</li><li>Physics props fall and jump forces feel appropriate under the selected gravity.</li><li>Music resolves to a valid Godot resource path, begins once, and stops according to the level's ownership.</li><li>Load events do not fire twice during development reloading.</li><li>Unload events do not reference nodes that have already been removed.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>FPE reads scene-level properties from the GLB, establishes the corresponding Godot environment and gravity, queues background music, and dispatches the authored load/unload command lists. Artists can own the level's mood and opening beat; engineers remain responsible for global save state, cross-level systems, and any rules that must outlive the scene.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-scene-settings.html">Read the complete FPE scene settings reference</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
